{"name":"Celadon Blue","hex":"#007ba7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"celadon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pale green colour, possibly tinted with gray.","A pale green Chinese glaze."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a pale green colour tinted with gray."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/celadon"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Celadon","blocks":[{"text":"Celadon is a term for pottery denoting both wares glazed in the jade green celadon color, also known as greenware or \"green ware\", and a type of transparent glaze, often with small cracks, that was first used on greenware, but later used on other porcelains. Celadon originated in China, though the term is purely European, and notable kilns such as the Longquan kiln in Zhejiang province are renowned for their celadon glazes. Celadon production later spread to other parts of East Asia, such as Japan and Korea, as well as Southeast Asian countries, such as Thailand and Vietnam. Eventually, European potteries produced some pieces, but it was never a major element there. Finer pieces are in porcelain, but both the color and the glaze can be produced in stoneware and earthenware. Most of the earlier Longquan celadon is on the border of stoneware and porcelain, meeting the Chinese but not the European definitions of porcelain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Song_Dynasty_Celadon_Vase.jpg/330px-Song_Dynasty_Celadon_Vase.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celadon","wikidata":"Q1054559"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"},{"title":"Shades of green","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color green may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a green or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Shades_of_Green.svg/330px-Shades_of_Green.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_green","wikidata":"Q19847180"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Boddhisattva_Guanyin%2C_China%2C_Ming_dynasty%2C_1300-1400_AD%2C_porcelain_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09644.JPG/500px-Boddhisattva_Guanyin%2C_China%2C_Ming_dynasty%2C_1300-1400_AD%2C_porcelain_-_%C3%96stasiatiska_museet%2C_Stockholm_-_DSC09644.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaig